From 2665aaaa67d4a0e8a6c49be76f4d13527d86978e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicole@muzz.in>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:46:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] selection: mouse selection and painting;
 caretPositionFromPoint and caretRangeFromPoint

Text could not be selected with the mouse anywhere, and a selection made by
script was only painted inside the focused editing host.

Layout now records the DOM origin of all laid out text, not only editable text,
so caret positions can be found and selections painted in any text; the
caret stops query takes any node. Pressing the primary button selects like
Chrome on Linux: a click collapses the selection at the closest caret
position (Shift extends it), a double click selects the word, run of white
space or other character after that position, a triple click selects the
paragraph with the line break ending it, or up to the next block, and moving
the mouse with the button pressed extends the selection. Text controls keep
their own selection (the document selection moves before them), presses on buttons and
draggable elements (links, images) leave the selection alone, so they can be
dragged instead, a selection that starts
in an editing host stays in it (going to its start or end when dragged above
or below it) and one that starts outside editing hosts ends at their boundary.
Positions in the user agent shadow trees of text controls are never used.

The selection is painted wherever it is, over the whole line box, in Chrome's
colours (rgb(51, 103, 209) behind white text) unless a ::selection rule sets
them; selected glyphs are painted once in the selection colour, clipped to the
highlight, and once in the text colour on each side. Selected white space with
no glyphs is highlighted too. Script hands layout the selected text nodes by
walking the range once instead of comparing every text node with its ends.

An empty editing host (no children at all) is laid out with one line, as in
Chrome, so it has a height, a painted caret and a caret position for clicks;
typing into it works.

Consecutive ArrowUp/ArrowDown presses keep aiming for the horizontal position
where the first one started, as long as the caret is where the last one left
it, so moving through a short line no longer loses the column.

CaretPositionFromPoint and caretRangeFromPoint:

document.caretPositionFromPoint() and the WebKit caretRangeFromPoint() did not
exist. Monaco hit tests clicks with them, so clicking on code put the caret
nowhere (focus went to the body).

Add CaretPosition and caretPositionFromPoint() from CSSOM View, using the
element at the point and the closest laid out caret position in the nearest
block around it, or the text index for text controls. caretRangeFromPoint()
returns the same position as a collapsed range, before the control for text
controls, like Blink.

